{"input_text": "Question: Given  $f(x)=ax+b+\frac{c}{x}$  and  $N$ , I'd like to ask how to calculate  $\sum_{i=1}^{N}f(x)^i$  efficiently using fast Fourier transform? 

Answer: Let's solve the problem in a much easier manner. Denote the probability of winning the game after  $n$  times playing Craps by  $p_n$ . Therefore $$p_n{=\Pr(\text{Winning at the n-th chance})\\=\Pr(\text{Postponing the first n-1 times and} \\\ \ \ \text{ winning the last one})\\=\Big(\Pr(\text{The sum takes on values 4,5,6,8,9,10})\Big)^{n-1}\\\ \ \ \cdot \Pr(\text{The sum possesses the values 7,11})}$$ From the other side $$\Pr(\text{The sum takes on values 4,5,6,8,9,10})=1-{8\over 36}-{4\over 36}={2\over 3}\\\Pr(\text{The sum possesses the values 7,11})={2\over 9}$$ therefore  $$p_n=\left({2\over 3}\right)^{n-1}\cdot {2\over 9}$$ so we can write  $$\Pr(\text{Winning at some time})=\sum_{n=1}^\infty p_n={2\over 9}\cdot{1\over 1-{2\over 3}}={2\over 3}$$ 
