Answer: Any number  $n\in\mathbb{N}$  is between  $10^k \leq n < 10^{k+1}$  for some  $k\in\mathbb{N}$ . This means that the number of digits of  $n$  is  $k+1$ . When you take  $\log_{10}$  of all sides, you get  $k \leq \log{n} < k+1$ , which basically gives you  $k+1$  if you take the floor of  $\log{n}$  and add  $1$ , i.e.,  $\lfloor\log{n}\rfloor+1$ . 
